IngeTrazo — authors and contributors
=====================================

Creator and lead author
------------------------
Marco Sumari Tellez <ing.sumari@gmail.com>
    Civil Engineer, Arequipa, Peru.
    Original author and designer of IngeTrazo; wrote the topology engine,
    tools, rendering, and file formats.

Contributors
-------------
★ marks those who have given the most (measured by merged pull requests,
reports and the work they carried); they also come first in Help ▸ About.

★ Pedro Caeiro <pacaeiro2@sapo.pt>  (github.com/pacaeiro)
    Architectural and mechanical draftsman.
    Five merged pull requests (#11, #13, #15, #17, #21): the protractor
    shortcut conflict with pan, the exact axis Shift lock, opt-in larger
    toolbar icons, guides kept dashed by clipping to the window, and snapping
    to intersections between guides.
    Fifteen issues reported from daily drafting work (#7–#26) — mirrored
    orbit, snap points inside groups, axis lock accuracy, the protractor's
    click-drag, Move with Ctrl, self-inference, guide-from-guide, guides
    drawn in front of geometry, rotate-copy arrays, and the Alt key
    colliding with Alt+Tab. Much of how IngeTrazo's everyday gestures feel
    comes from him using the program as a draftsman does and writing down
    every one that did not answer as expected.
    He kept going through 0.4 and 0.5 (#27–#106): axis locks and their
    snaps, local axes in groups and components (#44), the sheet composer,
    Fillet 3D, Orient Faces, copy/paste between documents, and many more —
    and a pull request of his own for the composer (#116): a resized view
    keeps its drawing undistorted and renders again off the release.
    Two more for 0.5.4: a moved view catches vertices where it is (#124)
    and a centred rectangle's square cue is a real square (#126).
    And for 0.5.9: a typed length drops the axis lock (#568), stale
    inference references cleared after undo (#556), the arrows and Shift
    locking the drawing plane (#634, #608), planar alignment on selected
    geometry (#690), first-point axis snaps for the drawing tools (#718),
    camera-aware planar previews (#719) and switching orbit and pan during a
    middle-button drag (#720). Over a hundred issues filed in all.
    And for 0.5.10: Shift and the arrow locks steering a dimension's
    placement step (#770, #810).
    And for 0.5.11: a locked line that finds where it crosses an edge or a
    face (#245), and Scale… with X, Y and Z for groups and components
    (#771).
    And for 0.6.0: Iso snapping to the nearest isometric view (#995), 3D
    text that no longer sinks into faces (#1057) and the Circle snapping to
    the axes again (#962, #992).

★ Rafael García Rodríguez — "Rafael 3D" (youtube.com/@Rafa3D)
    Draftsman and 3D modelling reviewer.
    Hour-long filmed reviews of IngeTrazo, walking the program as a
    professional would and narrating every friction — the source of the
    sheet-composer work and of dozens of fixes. Diagnosed the NVIDIA +
    Wayland startup failure on his own machine.
    Above all, the drafting-STANDARDS tutorial: twenty minutes and a
    reference plate drawn in AutoCAD setting out how dimensions must be laid
    out under ISO and under the German/Japanese norm — text above the line,
    read counter-clockwise, diameters through the centre, radii from it. A
    written specification for something the project had no way to get right
    on its own. In his words: «a software can be very powerful but it can
    all go to waste if it does not meet the standards designers need».

★ Ahsan Mehmood <mail.ahsanmehmood@gmail.com>  (github.com/iamahsanmehmood)
    Author of OpenSKP, the clean-room pure-Python .skp reader IngeTrazo's
    native .skp import is built on, and reviewer of the patches contributed
    back upstream. His plugin pull requests (#1–#3) became the Extensions
    system.

dafrobozao <dafrobozao.93@gmail.com>  (github.com/dafrobozao)
    Brazilian Portuguese translation of the interface (pull request #54,
    1 260 strings): IngeTrazo's third language, and the first one
    contributed from outside.

★ Félix Riestra  (github.com/felixriestra)
    The macOS package (pull request #64): the IngeTrazo.app bundle and the
    .dmg built by CI for Apple Silicon, with double-click opening of .igz
    and .skp files. IngeTrazo's first Mac version.
    Components that come apart (pull request #96): OBJ files keep their
    parts, Split into Pieces, the Parts tray with its cut list, and the
    exploded view — furniture and joinery taken to pieces.
    Extension API 2 (pull request #141): several named panels per
    extension, overlays that project world points, file types an extension
    opens itself, and workspaces that stand in for the model.

★ Gabriel Rodríguez Arregin  (github.com/canalsecuario-blip)
    The Back color in Styles (pull request #99): the Front/Back colour
    pair, the tint of faces seen from behind.
    Edit ▸ Invert Selection (pull request #113), on Ctrl+Shift+I, leaving
    out what is hidden or locked.
    Edit ▸ Repeat Last Command (Shift+R): the last tool or one-shot
    command comes back on the current selection.
    Command search (pull request #168): F3 runs any command by name, as in
    Blender — initials, typos and both languages, in the model and the
    sheet composer.
    The Browser: the model's groups and components as a tree (the
    Outliner, built with Carlos Martins's plugin, #87, on Roy Méndez
    Castro's scope, #219), and Views and sheets in the sheet composer.
    The units system: one core to show and read every measure, the Units
    panel (Ctrl+U), a choice at startup and 3D Text in the document's
    unit. Fillet 3D bevels, profiles and corners; the Solid Inspector,
    repairing problem by problem; Join Segments with its preview; the
    Browser's make, explode, make unique and batch rename (#642); floating
    toolbars that look like a window (#670), Restore default toolbars and
    panels (#668, #677), tooltips with the name in bold (#667), the left
    sidebar shortcut (#662) and About ▸ Copy to clipboard (#674).
    For 0.5.10: Camera ▸ Axes (Alt+A) to switch the drawing axes off;
    guides moved, copied and rotated like the rest of the model (#627);
    zoom that comes within 5 mm of a small part (#653, #692); a flat tilted
    circle far from the origin planar again (#571); Make Group → Explode
    keeping both redo steps (#737); survey-coordinate files imported near
    the origin, undistorted (#798); smooth curved surfaces and named
    materials in GLB, DAE and OBJ export (#131, #794).
    For 0.5.11: Layers 2.0 (#652, #728) — the Layers tab with eye, lock,
    isolate, active layer, colour, line type and weight, transparency and
    printing per layer, folders and the Layers toolbar; Bridge between two
    faces or lines (#614, #683); groups that leave no ghost geometry
    behind (#987, #988); a drawn circle's or arc's sides changed in place
    (#813); dimensions without their unit (#374, #741); renaming the
    active material (#806); the "+" after the sidebar tabs (#797); whole
    circles after copy and explode (#812); and the faded rest of the model
    reaching images and face-me figures (#805).
    For 0.6.0: the softer ambient occlusion with horizons and bounced light
    (#1060, #1061) and no more vertical bands on grazing faces (#989, #991);
    curved surfaces that shade round (#131, #809); the sheet composer in a
    window of its own (#1068, #1069); the Browser that sorts, folds and
    filters (#734, #1002); locking groups from the right-click (#782,
    #1047); and Restore defaults in Preferences (#1052).

José Castro Basso — FADU–UDELAR, Montevideo  (github.com/castrobasso)
    Architect and teacher of architectural representation. Two-point
    perspective with vertical verticals and File ▸ Export ▸ Current view as
    DXF, from his own modified build; the Levels extension (levels with
    guides and snapping in elevations), whose branch opened IngeTrazo to
    extensions that keep data in the document, add a panel and offer the
    cursor an inference. Building a free-software workflow for teaching:
    model → represent → edit graphically → deliver.

Sherod Taylor  (github.com/sherodtaylor)
    The First Person tool (pull request #86): walking the model like a
    game with W/A/S/D and mouse look, beside the Walk tool.

liuandy  (github.com/liujvnes)
    Simplified Chinese translation of the interface (issue #123, some
    2 080 strings): IngeTrazo's fourth language.

deedend  (github.com/deedend)
    Italian translation of the interface (pull request #182, some
    2 110 strings): IngeTrazo's fifth language.
    For 0.6.0: the 3D mouse with configurable axes and navigation profiles
    (#581).

Rainjalin  (github.com/Rainjalin)
    Indonesian translation of the interface (issue #216, some 2 230
    strings): IngeTrazo's sixth language.

Bane Andreev  (Biro Andreev, Belgrade / Zagreb)
    Architect. The Windowizer example extension: parametric windows from
    faces, one container per window with its frame and panes, and the wall
    opened and closed again — a port of Rick Wilson's Windowizer 3
    (2004-2005, whose notice it keeps). His notes on the extension API gave
    it add_menu, add_context_menu and per-group extension data.

Rony Leonel Janampa Monago  (github.com/Ronyleonel6)
    The Polygon properties example extension (issue #229, MIT): area,
    centroid and second moments of a face, for sections and slabs.

Carlos Martins  (github.com/fafecm)
    Bug reports with screen recordings that made each one reproducible:
    diagonal guides crossing a hair off (#110), a group's paint missing
    from the materials in the model (#133), groups drawn out of place
    after exploding (#134).
    The Project Structure plugin (#87), shared with its code: the tree of
    groups mirrored with the view both ways, the eye per node and nested
    groups entered through their path, re-nesting by drag and drop and a
    padlock per group — the base of the Outliner, which now keeps the lock
    in the document.

mnavarromugas  (github.com/mnavarromugas)
    The first to try the 3D mouse on a real 3Dconnexion SpaceMouse (#108)
    and to suggest inverting each axis on its own.

xyont  (github.com/xyont)
    Reports from mechanical modelling in millimetres: pulling a face up
    against a wall (#94), units kept for new documents (#121), colours by
    layer (#120).

Roy Méndez Castro  (github.com/roymendezcastro)
    Civil engineer, Costa Rica. Wrote down the Outliner (#219): the
    minimum a tree of nested groups needs, what comes after it, and that
    scenes must remember what it hides.

kagehak  (github.com/kagehak)
    STL import (pull request #161): binary and ASCII, the model's unit,
    flat surfaces merged on import, the reading off the window's thread,
    and Edit ▸ Simplify Mesh for any mesh.
    The extension manager (pull request #464): the community catalogue
    browsed, installed and updated from inside the program.

Orian Beltrame  (github.com/orianbsilva)
    Performance, measured: hovering with Line and Move up to twice as fast
    (#312), the pick index split into grid cells so a ray tests only the
    cells it crosses (#346), scene recalls and First Person that no longer
    re-sync the whole model (#360, #361), the interaction benchmark that
    times what the hand on the mouse feels (#308) and the fast suite
    passing on Windows (#288).

FG-RVT  (github.com/FG-RVT)
    Push/Pull usability (pull requests #455, #487): a preselected face
    arms the tool, the release commits the drag, the distance stays ready
    to retype, a dashed line shows what it is locked onto, and the drag
    previews the real result on split faces.

Guy Wydouw  (github.com/GWydouw)
